<h2>The FIR and the cross-complaint</h2>
<p>The FIR of 17 August 2026 alleged that the applicant, whom the complainant met through social media in August 2025, promised marriage, drugged her food, had non-consensual relations with her and secretly recorded an intimate video; money was allegedly taken from her and her family. The defence record had its own chronology: the applicant had filed a cross-complaint alleging extortion on 9 August 2026 — before the FIR — and had travelled abroad before its registration. The Sessions Court dismissed his anticipatory bail plea on 26 September 2026, bringing the matter to the High Court.</p>

<h2>The Court&rsquo;s calculus</h2>
<p>The order works through the factors the Supreme Court laid down in <strong>Siddharam Satlingappa Mhetre</strong>: the nature of the accusation, the applicant&rsquo;s conduct, the risk of flight, and the actual need for custodial interrogation. Four features of the record proved decisive. No notice under Section 35 BNSS had been served on the applicant — the graduated, notice-first procedure that the law prescribes before arrest in many cases had not been attempted. His parents had joined the investigation. The complainant&rsquo;s statement under Section 183 BNSS was already on record, reducing the investigative work that custody could advance. And the timeline — a relationship running from November 2025, an FIR in August 2026, preceded by the applicant&rsquo;s own extortion complaint — called for investigation rather than incarceration. The Court invoked the governing maxims: <em>&ldquo;arrest should be the last option&rdquo;</em> and <em>&ldquo;custodial interrogation should be avoided&rdquo;</em> where cooperation is forthcoming.</p>

<h2>Section 69 BNS and pre-arrest protection</h2>
<p>Section 69 occupies contested terrain: it protects against sexual exploitation by deception, while its breadth makes it a frequent entrant in soured-relationship disputes. Anticipatory bail jurisprudence supplies the filter. Where the relationship was long-running and documented, where the complaint follows a financial or matrimonial fallout, and where the accused demonstrates cooperation, courts lean toward protecting liberty and letting the investigation test the allegations. Where there is evidence of calculated deception, absconding or tampering, protection is refused — as other benches of the same court have done in appropriate cases.</p>
<div class="check">
  <p>Preserve the relationship&rsquo;s documentary trail — messages, call records, travel and financial transactions — since chronology is usually the strongest defence material in Section 69 cases.</p>
  <p>Respond to any Section 35 BNSS notice scrupulously; cooperation recorded in writing becomes the backbone of the anticipatory bail plea.</p>
  <p>Expect conditions that reach digital evidence: surrender of passport, access to devices and accounts, and location services are now routine in such orders.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">Anticipatory bail under Section 482 BNSS protects liberty pending investigation; it neither suspends the investigation nor forecasts its outcome. Every case under Section 69 BNS turns on its own record, and the complainant&rsquo;s allegations in this FIR remain sub judice.</div>
